<WovenDocs::Page
  @id="index"
  @title="WovenUI Ember kit"
  @subtitle="Glimmer components over the WovenUI SCSS component layer."
>
  <WovenDocs::Section @title="Overview" @id="overview">
    <p class="woven-doc__intro">
      The kit is one vocabulary in three packages: core tokens and
      utilities, the WovenUI CSS kit, and the Ember components that render
      its markup and own its state. Every component whitelists its variants, falls back to the
      default on a typo, spreads
      <code class="wu-code">...attributes</code>
      last on its root, and carries
      <code class="wu-code">data-test-wu-*</code>
      hooks on the root and on every interactive child.
    </p>
  </WovenDocs::Section>

  <WovenDocs::Section @title="Features" @id="features">
    <ul class="wu-list sm" role="list">
      <li class="wu-list-item"><span>Fifty-odd Glimmer components over one CSS kit vocabulary, each documented with every variant live.</span></li>
      <li class="wu-list-item"><span>One theme variable reskins the whole kit; per-component tokens retheme one instance.</span></li>
      <li class="wu-list-item"><span>Right-to-left at both layers, verified with the Dir switch on every page.</span></li>
      <li class="wu-list-item"><span>Four hosts and their services handle everything that floats.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </WovenDocs::Section>

  <WovenDocs::Section @title="Packages" @id="packages">
    <WovenDocs::Table @kind="api" @rows={{this.packages}} />
  </WovenDocs::Section>

  <WovenDocs::Section @title="Install" @id="install">
    <WovenDocs::Example
      @title="Styles"
      @note="Core first, then the kit. The kit entry forwards the token engine, loads the CSS kit bundle once with unused components switched off, then the Ember-only parts, utilities last."
      @layout="block"
      @code={{this.code.styles}}
    >
      <Woven::Text
        @size="sm"
        @muted={{true}}
        @text="Both sheets compile into one stylesheet; no runtime CSS is injected."
      />
    </WovenDocs::Example>

    <WovenDocs::Example
      @title="Mount the hosts"
      @note="Four hosts render once in the application template, after the routed content and in this order. Menus, popovers and tooltips render into the body layer, or into the layer of the open dialog that contains their trigger."
      @layout="block"
      @code={{this.code.hosts}}
    >
      <ul class="wu-list sm" role="list">
        <li class="wu-list-item"><span>Modals and drawers are native dialogs in the browser top layer; each carries its own wu-layer, because a modal dialog makes the body layer inert.</span></li>
        <li class="wu-list-item"><span>LayerHost before TooltipHost, so a tooltip on a menu item paints above the menu.</span></li>
        <li class="wu-list-item"><span>Toasts sit one z-index level higher, above everything.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </WovenDocs::Example>
  </WovenDocs::Section>

  <WovenDocs::Section @title="Theming" @id="theming">
    <WovenDocs::Example
      @note="Modifiers reassign CSS variables; the base rule is the only consumer. Change --wu-brand and every brand surface follows. Retheme one instance by overriding its own --wu-{block}--{Property} token from outside."
      @layout="block"
      @code={{this.code.theme}}
    >
      <Woven::Text
        @size="sm"
        @muted={{true}}
        @text="The Theme switch in the topbar sets --wu-brand on the html element. Try it: every primary button, badge, toggle and focus ring on this site follows."
      />
    </WovenDocs::Example>
  </WovenDocs::Section>

  <WovenDocs::Section @title="Right-to-left" @id="rtl">
    <WovenDocs::Example
      @note="The kit runs under a dir attribute on the html element with no per-use changes. The SCSS layer is RTL on its own (logical properties, mirrored keyframes); the components add reading-direction arrow keys, LTR pinning for numeric inputs and mirrored directional icons."
      @layout="block"
      @code={{this.code.rtl}}
    >
      <Woven::Text
        @size="sm"
        @muted={{true}}
        @text="The Dir switch in the topbar flips the whole site. Each component page lists its direction-specific behavior under RTL."
      />
    </WovenDocs::Example>
  </WovenDocs::Section>

  <WovenDocs::Section @title="Pages" @id="pages">
    {{#each this.layout.navSections as |group|}}
      <h3 class="woven-subtitle">{{group.label}}</h3>
      <nav class="woven-toc" aria-label={{group.label}}>
        {{#each group.items as |entry|}}
          <LinkTo
            @route={{entry.route}}
            class="woven-toc__link"
            data-test-woven-toc={{entry.route}}
          >
            {{entry.label}}
          </LinkTo>
        {{/each}}
      </nav>
    {{/each}}
  </WovenDocs::Section>
</WovenDocs::Page>
